import os, sys
out = sys.argv[1]
head = '''You are making game art for "Pause" (Unity, vertical 2D shooter, phone portrait, the player flies UP the screen). Work ONLY inside this directory tree; do not edit any .cs/.meta/.json or any existing file; do not delete anything; do not commit.
New files go ONLY to ./Pause/Assets/Art/Resources/ShipArt/Paranoid/src~/codex/ (the trailing ~ matters: Unity ignores it). Put helpers in its tools/ subfolder.
Read ./docs/art-style.md first (neon pixel art). The player ship is "Paranoid", redrawn in hi-res neon pixel art: a leaf-green faceted disc hull with a teal hex canopy, a red diamond light on top and an amber one on the bottom, and two tall engine pods with red cap bands and amber nozzle lips.

BACKGROUND KEY: the stock ship is green, so every image here uses a flat pure MAGENTA #FF00FF background (never green). The ship itself must contain no magenta/pink.

REFERENCES (attached images, in order): (1) skinref_state0.png, (2) skinref_state1.png, (3) skinref_state2.png: the FINAL approved flipbook, three 3 x 3 sheets (intact, damaged, wreck), 9 poses each in the order key pose / bob up / bob up lights off / glint 1 / glint 2 lights off / bob down / bank left / bank right / hit flash. Each is 1536 x 1536 (cells 512 px).
RULES: no pink or magenta anywhere on the ship. No flames. No outline or halo around the ship. Flat pure #FF00FF background.
'''
body = '''
JOB SKIN-{name}: a REPAINTED skin of the finished flipbook. Redraw each of the three sheets in the "{name}" skin and write skin_{name}_state0.png, skin_{name}_state1.png, skin_{name}_state2.png (same 3 x 3 layout, each cell 1024 x 1024 i.e. 3072 x 3072 per file, flat #FF00FF background, every pose at the SAME position and scale as the reference sheet). THE DRAWING MUST STAY IDENTICAL cell for cell (same silhouette, panel lines, damage on both pods and the disc, cracks, glints, bank perspective, light dashes): ONLY THE ARMOUR PAINT CHANGES:
{rules}
Everything else (canopy glass, engine bells and nozzle glow, ink edge, glints, and ALL damage effects: sparks, glowing orange tears, embers, soot, exposed machinery) stays as in the reference. The hit-flash cell stays bone-white. Heavier damage rows keep their full heaviness (row 3 must still look like a wreck over the whole hull, row 2 clearly lighter).
This is a real REPAINT, not a recolour: re-shade the plating in the new material with its own 5-6 tone ramp, new highlight placement on the plate edges, fresh panel-seam shading, small paint details (scuffs, edge wear, rivets glints), exactly the care of the original painting; do NOT just shift the hue/saturation of the reference. Use IMAGE GENERATION to repaint each sheet from the reference (several candidates, pick the truest to the drawing), then clean with nearest-neighbour; every pixel edge crisp, no blur, no translucent overlays.
Write tools/check_skin_{name}.py that, for each of the three files and each of the 9 cells, prints: silhouette (non-magenta mask) difference vs the reference cell (must be < 1.5%), difference over the canopy/teal region (must be < 6%), and the fraction of all pixels that changed (must be > {chg}% so it is visibly a new skin). It must also print a pixel-level "texture" check: the mean absolute difference between the luminance of this skin and the reference luminance inside the armour (must be > 6 grey levels for the palette skins, proving it is a re-shade and not a plain hue shift). FIX AND REDRAW until every number passes; print the summary in your final message. Finish with honest caveats.
'''
skins = {
    'Night': ('''- the leaf-green ARMOUR (all green plating, with its darker shading and pale-green highlights) becomes deep midnight indigo-navy metal: base #2A2E6B, shadow #1A1F45, rim-light highlights bright cyan #6EF2EE along the upper-left edges of the plates (neon edge lighting) with a few pale blue-white highlights. Keep the red cap bands, the diamond lights and the canopy as they are.''', 12),
    'Sodium': ('''- the leaf-green ARMOUR becomes warm sodium-lamp ORANGE metal: base #F2862B, shadow #A9481A, highlights #FFB43C with pale yellow-white hot edges. Keep the diamond lights and the canopy as they are; the red cap bands stay red.''', 12),
    'Bone': ('''- the leaf-green ARMOUR becomes warm BONE-white ceramic armour: base #F4EAD4, shadow #B9AE98, highlights near-white, plate seams in a slightly darker warm grey. The pod cap bands, the top diamond light and the bottom diamond light become strong RED (#D8232C with a hot #FF5B45 core) so they still read on the pale hull. The canopy stays teal.''', 12),
    'Signal': ('''- this is the special DIAGONAL LIVERY. Keep the leaf-green armour exactly as it is (same green shading), and paint ONE crisp straight diagonal BAND across the whole ship, running from the UPPER-LEFT to the LOWER-RIGHT at exactly 45 degrees, as one continuous band across the left pod, the disc and the right pod (it passes through the centre of the disc; the canopy glass and the two diamond lights are drawn on top of the band and stay unpainted). The band is solid signal amber #FFB43C, perpendicular width about 140 px in the 1024 px cell (about 14% of the cell), with a darker orange #F2862B lower-right edge strip about 20 px wide, and a thin warm bone #F4EAD4 highlight line about 6 px wide along its upper-left edge. Its edges are perfectly straight and clean stair-stepped pixel lines (no mottling, no ragged patches, no feathering), it follows the plate relief (plate seams and rivets drawn over it, damage drawn on top of it so cracks, scorch, holes and blown panels cut through it exactly as in the reference), and it covers only the armour. In the bank-left / bank-right cells the band follows the same perspective as the hull. Nothing else on the ship changes colour (so the fraction changed is smaller here). The hit-flash cell stays bone-white.''', 3),
}
for k, (rules, chg) in skins.items():
    open(os.path.join(out, 'skin_%s.md' % k), 'w').write(head + body.format(name=k, rules=rules, chg=chg))
